        <!-- SECTION 2: Duty Ratings and Reach -->
        <div id="ratings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Duty Ratings and Reach
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two numbers determine whether a ladder suits your job, and both are routinely misread.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Duty rating includes everything, not just you.</strong> A 250 pound rating covers your body weight plus clothing, tools, and whatever material you are carrying up. A 200 pound person with a bag of tools and a bundle of guttering is closer to that limit than they expect.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Type IA at 300 pounds is the sensible default.</strong> It gives realistic headroom for a person plus equipment. Type II at 250 is adequate for light indoor work and Type IAA at 375 is what heavier trades specify.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Working height is not ladder length.</strong> You cannot safely stand on the top few rungs, and the ladder must lean at an angle rather than stand vertical. A twelve foot ladder gives a working reach of roughly fifteen feet allowing for arm extension.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The four to one angle rule costs you height.</strong> A ladder should stand one foot out from the wall for every four feet of height, which means a twelve foot ladder reaches a point slightly under twelve feet up the wall. Standing it more vertically to gain reach is how ladders tip backwards.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Extending above a roof edge matters.</strong> If you are climbing onto a roof, the ladder must extend around three feet above the edge to give something to hold while transitioning. Factor that into the length you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy longer than you think.</strong> The most common regret with ladders is buying one that just reaches, because just reaching means working at the top of it, which is exactly where it is least safe. A ladder that reaches comfortably is used comfortably.
          </p>
        </div>

