        <!-- SECTION 4: The Retraction Risk and Ladder Safety -->
        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Retraction Risk and Ladder Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Telescoping ladders introduce one hazard that conventional ladders do not, and it accounts for most injuries associated with them.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Retraction traps fingers.</strong> Releasing the locks lets sections drop, and a hand resting on a rung when the section above comes down is exactly the injury these ladders are known for. It happens quickly and it is serious.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hold the rails, not the rungs, during retraction.</strong> Keep hands on the outside of the rails where nothing can close on them, release the locks in sequence from the top, and let each section down deliberately rather than releasing several at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy a ladder with controlled retraction.</strong> Better ladders damp the descent or use a sequenced release, which removes most of the risk. This is worth paying for and it is the main functional difference between price tiers.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Confirm every lock before climbing.</strong> Look along the ladder and check each indicator. A partially engaged lock can hold under no load and give way under yours, and that is the failure that causes falls.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Set it at the correct angle on level ground.</strong> One foot out for every four feet of height, on a firm level surface, with the feet properly seated. Soft ground, decking gaps and sloped surfaces all need attention before you climb.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep your belt buckle between the rails.</strong> Overreaching sideways is the most common cause of ladder falls. If you have to lean past the rails, climb down and move the ladder. It takes twenty seconds and it is the whole difference.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Hands on the Rails, Always</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              The one habit that prevents the injury telescoping ladders are known for: during retraction, keep both hands on the outside of the rails and never on the rungs. Release locks from the top downward and let each section settle before releasing the next. Sections that drop unexpectedly cannot catch a hand that is not in their path.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: What to Look for When Buying -->
        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What to Look for When Buying
          </h2>

          <p>
            Beyond reach and rating, a handful of features separate a ladder you trust from one you approach warily.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clear lock indicators.</strong> Coloured markers that show engaged or not engaged at a glance mean you can verify the whole ladder in two seconds from the ground, rather than assuming.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Controlled or sequenced retraction.</strong> Any mechanism that slows or orders the collapse addresses the main hazard of the format. Ladders that simply drop when released are the ones that cause injuries.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wide, grippy feet.</strong> The contact patch at the bottom is where stability comes from. Broad rubber feet that pivot to sit flat on the ground make a substantial difference on hard surfaces, and any ladder used on soft ground needs a board underneath.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Rung width and profile.</strong> Wider rungs with a flat top are dramatically more comfortable underfoot over a long job than narrow round ones, which is worth checking if you will be standing on it for extended periods.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>ANSI or EN certification, verified.</strong> A duty rating printed on a sticker without a corresponding standard means little. Look for an ANSI type rating or an EN 131 mark, both of which involve actual testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Fibreglass if there is any electrical risk.</strong> Aluminium conducts, and overhead service drops are closer to gutters than people assume. If your work goes anywhere near live conductors, the material is not a preference.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Telescoping, Multi Position or Extension -->
        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Telescoping, Multi Position or Extension
          </h2>

          <p>
            Telescoping is one of three formats competing for the same jobs, and the right answer depends almost entirely on where the ladder will live.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Telescoping wins on storage and transport.</strong> Collapsing to roughly a third of its length is what lets a ladder live in a cupboard, an apartment, or the boot of a car. If that is your constraint, nothing else in this category competes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Conventional extension ladders win on rigidity and price.</strong> Two large sections with a rope and pawl flex far less, cost considerably less for the same reach, and have almost nothing to go wrong. They also need a garage wall and a vehicle with a roof rack.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Multi-position ladders win on versatility.</strong> Hinged joints convert between extension, A-frame, stairway and platform configurations, covering jobs a straight ladder cannot attempt. They fold rather than collapse, so they are bulkier, and they are heavy.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Stepladders remain the right tool indoors.</strong> Free standing, no wall required, and stable on a flat floor. A great deal of household ladder use is genuinely a stepladder job, and using a leaning ladder indoors because it is what you own is a common source of trouble.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Consider how often you will actually use it.</strong> A ladder used twice a year that lives somewhere accessible gets used properly. One that needs dragging out from behind the garden furniture encourages standing on a chair instead, which is how most household falls happen.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Two ladders is often the honest answer.</strong> A compact telescoping ladder for the frequent short jobs and a longer rigid ladder for annual work at height cost less combined than one compromise ladder that does neither job comfortably.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Maintenance and Storage -->
        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Maintenance and Storage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Telescoping ladders have moving parts exposed to weather and grit, which makes maintenance more relevant than for a conventional ladder.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep the sections clean.</strong> Grit and dried mud between nested tubes prevents sections sliding and, worse, prevents locks seating fully. Wiping the rails down after use takes a minute and prevents the most common cause of stiff or unreliable operation.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dry it before collapsing it.</strong> Water trapped inside nested sections encourages corrosion and freezes in winter, which can lock a ladder solid. Let it drain and dry before storing collapsed.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not lubricate with oil.</strong> Oil attracts grit and turns it into a grinding paste inside the sections. A dry silicone or PTFE spray is the correct product where movement becomes stiff.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Inspect the locks before each use.</strong> Look for bent tabs, sluggish engagement, or an indicator that does not change properly. Any lock behaving differently from the others is a reason to stop using the ladder until it is resolved.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Store it horizontally or hanging, not leaning.</strong> A collapsed ladder stood in a corner can slip and fall, and long term pressure on the bottom section can distort it. Hooks on a garage wall solve both.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check the feet for wear.</strong> Rubber ladder feet harden and wear smooth over years, and a smooth foot on a smooth surface slides at exactly the wrong moment. Replacement feet are inexpensive on most brands and are among the cheapest safety improvements available.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Retire a ladder that has been dropped from height.</strong> Aluminium sections that have taken an impact can be subtly bent in a way that binds or weakens without being visible. Given what a ladder is holding up, replacement is the correct response.
          </p>
        </div>

