        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Telescoping Ladder Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Telescoping ladders are safe used properly, and the mistakes that cause injuries are consistent and avoidable.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Holding the Rungs During Retraction</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                This is the injury telescoping ladders are known for. Releasing a lock lets the section above drop onto whatever is below it, and a hand on a rung is exactly in that path. Keep both hands on the outside of the rails, release locks from the top down, and let each section settle before the next.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Checking Every Lock</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A partially engaged lock holds under no load and gives way under yours, which is the failure that causes falls. Look along the ladder from the ground and confirm every indicator before climbing. Partial extension is fine, but only when you have verified the locks below your working height.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Standing It Too Vertically</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The four to one angle rule exists because a ladder leaned too upright tips backwards, and the temptation to stand it steeper to gain a little reach is exactly how that happens. If the ladder does not reach at the correct angle, it is the wrong ladder for the job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overreaching Instead of Moving It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Leaning sideways past the rails shifts your centre of gravity outside the ladder's base and is the most common cause of ladder falls. Keeping your belt buckle between the rails is the standard rule. Climbing down and moving the ladder takes twenty seconds.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

